.TH LIFELINED 8 "2026-11-21" "lifeline 0.15.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
lifelined \- lifeline background service daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lifelined
.RB [ \-\-config
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-log\-level
.IR LEVEL ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lifelined
is the system daemon started by
.I lifeline.service
to monitor Wi-Fi connectivity, coordinate with other DHCP servers on
Ethernet, and manage Ethernet addressing,
.BR dnsmasq ,
and forwarding rules. Most operators should manage it via
.BR systemctl (1)
and
.BR lifelinectl (8)
rather than invoking it directly.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Path to configuration file (default:
.IR /etc/lifeline/config.toml ).
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-level " LEVEL"
Set log verbosity to
.BR DEBUG ,
.BR INFO " (default),"
.BR WARNING ,
or
.BR ERROR .
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload configuration and the MAC-to-IP table.
.TP
.B SIGTERM, SIGINT
Stop serving DHCP, block Ethernet forwarding, and restore system settings.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LIFELINE_LOG_LEVEL
Default log level when
.B \-\-log\-level
is not passed on the command line.
.SH LOGS
Logs are written to standard error and captured by
.BR systemd-journald .
Inspect them with:
.RS
.EX
journalctl -u lifeline -f
.EE
.RE
.SH AUTHOR
Vikram Aggarwal
.SH REPORTING BUGS AND CONTRIBUTING
Bug fixes and feature improvements are warmly encouraged. Include
.B journalctl -u lifeline -b
and
.B lifelinectl status \-\-json
when reporting issues or proposing enhancements.
